1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:04,800
[Mrs. Lan, in Vietnamese] “Hello, I’m Mrs. Lan, from the
Eastside. After the ice storm,
my house has been without
power for two days now.”

2
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:10,469
Mrs. Lan doesn't read English,
and her power's been out two days.

3
00:00:10,469 --> 00:00:13,900
She just left a voice
note. Watch the clock.

4
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:17,791
She heard it read back in
Vietnamese and said yes.

5
00:00:17,791 --> 00:00:23,623
The group's own rules, not the AI, set
the amount: a hundred and fifty dollars.

6
00:00:23,623 --> 00:00:25,100
Two organizers sign.

7
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:34,819
Paid, into her Venmo,
with a message in Vietnamese.

8
00:00:34,819 --> 00:00:39,980
Twenty-two point four seconds to the
SUCCESS webhook, on our PayPal stand-in.

9
00:00:39,980 --> 00:00:43,600
The approvals came on cue;
real neighbors take longer.

10
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:47,288
This is Neighborline:
a hotline, not a dashboard.

11
00:00:47,288 --> 00:00:51,861
After the Eaton fire, one LA mutual-aid
network took three hundred requests

12
00:00:51,861 --> 00:00:56,800
overnight, in Instagram comments. Most
still run on a volunteer's personal Venmo.

13
00:00:57,500 --> 00:01:01,800
Rosa asks for groceries, in Spanish.
Same rules, two approvals.

14
00:01:09,626 --> 00:01:13,000
Her email has no PayPal
account yet: unclaimed.

15
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:15,226
She gets claim help in Spanish.

16
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:19,662
She claims it. Success. Most
payout systems stop at unclaimed;

17
00:01:19,662 --> 00:01:21,300
this one follows through.

18
00:01:21,900 --> 00:01:26,730
Someone posing as the organizers,
with a Venmo handle one letter off?

19
00:01:26,730 --> 00:01:30,300
Approve is disabled on
every phone. No money moves.

20
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:35,547
Under the hood: the agents that talk
to neighbors have no PayPal tools.

21
00:01:35,547 --> 00:01:39,558
One separate approval service
holds the Payouts credentials,

22
00:01:39,558 --> 00:01:45,040
and it pays only on a record signed with
the passkeys of two different organizers.

23
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:50,400
AI output is schema-checked, repaired
once, or the neighbor is asked again.

24
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:56,256
The batch ID is derived from the signed
approval, so a double tap can't pay twice.

25
00:01:56,256 --> 00:02:01,447
Webhooks are signature-verified, and
every payout is reconciled against PayPal

26
00:02:01,447 --> 00:02:05,040
Transaction Search. The test
count is from a real run.

27
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:10,240
For agent builders: the same
guard ships as an MCP server.

28
00:02:10,240 --> 00:02:15,446
An AI agent asking to pay is refused
without two humans' signatures.

29
00:02:15,446 --> 00:02:16,900
With them, it pays.

30
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:23,261
Donors give with PayPal or Venmo.
The public report never names a neighbor,

31
00:02:23,261 --> 00:02:26,100
and every number comes from the ledger.

32
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:32,046
Neighborline is open source. Try the
hotline yourself; the link is below.

33
00:02:32,046 --> 00:02:37,300
Because the morning after a storm,
help should sound like your own language.
